WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:06.590
Hi, everyone. Welcome to the sixth
tutorial in our "Imaging Science Series"

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:10.520
here at the MIT Museum. For today's video,
we have a special guest,

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.820
Jeffrey Kuhn, who is the scientific
director of the Koch Institute's

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:19.999
Microscopy Core Facility here at MIT.
He's going to discuss what exactly is a

00:00:19.999 --> 00:00:24.859
digital image and how we can use that
knowledge to make adjustments to those

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:30.380
images and to show things that we
wouldn't have otherwise seen. With

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.940
that, I'm going hand it over to Jeffrey. [Jeffrey] In this video, we're going to be talking

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:39.350
about scientific images, some of the most
important features of them, and how you

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.560
process them. I'm going to give you a
little bit of background information

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.170
first, on what scientific images are and
some of the basic features that we are

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:52.130
going to need to understand in order to
bring out more information from these

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:56.280
images.
So, what are the main things we look for

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.350
in a scientific image? Well, the first
thing we look for is whether or not you

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.949
can see what you want to see. This is
dictated by the contrast; whether

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:10.710
something is visible in the first place.
We also want to know if we have enough

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:16.740
information to distinguish things
and that's resolution. We typically

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.860
think of resolution in terms of
megapixels in your camera, but there's a

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.000
scientific definition of resolution -- that
is, can you distinguish two things as

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.890
separate or not? There's also how much
uncertainty there is in your measurement;

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:37.560
how clear your image is. Every
measurement you take has uncertainty. If

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.950
you measure the temperature over the
course of a day, your uncertainty might

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:46.680
be a few degrees Fahrenheit. So, images
are no different. There is an uncertainty

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.509
in the measurement of intensities of an
image. In imaging, we use the

00:01:51.509 --> 00:01:55.530
signal-to-noise ratio. If noise is the
uncertainty in your measurement, the

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:00.060
signal is what you want to see, we tend
to measure the ratio between those two

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:06.360
things to tell us how clear our
certainty is. Then, finally, there's

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.560
the speed of which you take an
image. That's called temporal resolution.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.870
Did you capture the right moment in
time?

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:20.390
So, you can't have good contrast, good
resolution, good clarity, and fast speeds

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:25.430
all at once. There's always a compromise
between these factors. There's always a

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:29.599
balance you have to make and this is
called the "triangle of compromise." So, for

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:33.380
instance, if you want something that's
both bright and high resolution in

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:38.959
detail, you'll have to image very slowly.
If you want something that's very fast

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:43.160
imaging and high resolution, you won't be able to collect as much

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.060
light. It's going to be dimmer. If you
want something that's bright and fast,

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.620
you're going to have to sacrifice
spatial resolution, but you'll

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:57.200
end up with a blockier image.
We can, however, balance between these

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:03.410
factors. For most cameras, you can set the
exposure time for your images. So, if you

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:08.630
want to do a fast exposure to capture
fine motion, you can do that, but you'll

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:13.580
be getting a dimmer image and you might
have to sacrifice some detail. If you

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:18.530
want slower images, you can make them
brighter. Some cameras let you actually

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:22.250
change the resolution of the image,
basically putting a bunch of pixels

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:27.380
together into big super pixels. That
actually increases the sensor area the

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:32.090
pixels, makes them brighter. So, you can
get, at the expense of lower resolution,

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:35.360
you can get a brighter image. Or if you
want higher resolution, you're going to

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.459
deal with dimmer, or less light
collecting ability. Also, you can bounce

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:47.510
between these things by expense. So,
bigger sensors and bigger lenses

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:51.530
can collect more light, but that ends up
being more expensive. So, the camera

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:56.720
equipment that you use can dictate how
far along this "triangle of compromise"

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.390
you can go. So, digital image is
fundamentally a matrix of numbers that

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:06.980
describe intensities. So, we're going to
use this image of an old world ape mandrill

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:12.019
to talk about the numbers that make up
the intensities in an image. We're

00:04:12.019 --> 00:04:14.810
going to ignore color for the time being
and just look at a black and white

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:18.950
version of this mandrill. So, let's take a
look at a little tiny portion of the

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:23.510
muzzle -- near this little red line on the
muzzle. If we blow up that little

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:28.370
portion that looks like a bunch of gray
squares. Those gray squares

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:33.020
are made up of a bunch of numbers. If
you look, the squares on the left-hand

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:37.600
side tend to be a little bit higher
value -- let's say 180-200, things like that.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.169
Then those images are brighter, those
pixels are brighter. The ones on the

00:04:42.169 --> 00:04:47.570
right-hand side are a little bit lower
value -- 111, 118 they tend to be darker. So,

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:55.070
the numbers in this matrix describe
intensities. So, how do computers

00:04:55.070 --> 00:05:00.830
represent numbers? Well, we represent
numbers the Arabic way, or in powers of

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:07.340
10 and decimal digits. So, for instance, a
three-digit number has a ones place, a tens

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.560
place, and a hundreds place. For example, the number

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:14.390
136 is a one in a hundreds place, a three
in the tens place, and a six in the ones

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:18.140
place. You can sum all those together -- a
hundred, plus 30, plus six and that gives

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:25.490
you 136. Well, computers represent numbers
in a binary fashion. So, they use a single

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:30.980
number between zero or one to describe a
number. Each of these bits can go

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:35.030
from zero to one and they have different places -- a ones place, a twos place, a fours place,

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:39.650
etc. So, for instance, the number one can
be represented by a one in the ones

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:44.300
place and zero everywhere else. The
number 136 can be represented by a

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:48.650
number one in the 128 place and a one in
the 8th place and that adds together to

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:54.730
be 136. If you have all ones in one of
these eight bit values, the maximum number is

00:05:54.730 --> 00:06:04.580
255. So, eight bits can have a maximum of
255 and a minimum of zero. It turns out

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:09.500
the more bits you use in one of these
images, the more shades of gray you can

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:13.430
represent. So, for instance, two bits can
represent four different shades of gray.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:19.970
So, here's the mandrill with four shades
of gray. You can also use four bits, or 16

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:23.180
shades of gray.and now we're starting to
get a little bit more detail on this

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:30.140
mandrill. It doesn't look quite so chunky.
Eight bits gives you 256 shades of gray and

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:36.320
now this mandrill looks very smooth -- very
good detail in this mandrill. But

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.040
computers, and sometimes scientific
images, can actually take even more than

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:47.240
eight bits. They might have 16 bits of
of numbers in your image, so 65,000

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:52.970
shades. Or you might use color. Where in
most cases, most computers will use eight

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:59.630
bits per color -- so 256 shades of red, 256
shades of green, 256 shades of blue. Let's

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:05.120
look, now, at the difference between eight and
four bits. Let's zoom up on a little bit of

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.440
a portion of this. Now if you notice in
the four bit image, you can see the

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.040
gradations from gray to white in the
muzzle.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:17.450
Whereas with 256 shades, you can't really
see much of a gradation at all. It looks

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:21.710
much smoother. So, it turned out somewhere
between that 16 shades and

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:31.340
256 shades, is about where your eyes can
distinguish individual shades of gray. So,

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:36.380
what makes a good image? These are
examples of cells taken by microscopes

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:41.180
and most of these have slightly
different contrasts. It's the

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:45.050
contrast that makes them better or worse.
So, for example, the cells on the top

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:49.340
left-hand side, the top left and the top
middle, are taken with the same

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.910
microscope using different contrast
methods. In this top left image, you can

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:57.590
barely start to see some cells and, in
the middle image, you can actually make

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:00.260
them out because they've got higher
contrast.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:05.000
Similarly, this image of a cell on the
right hand side, has pretty good contrast

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.950
as well. Not quite as good as the one in
the middle. Then in the bottom row,

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:16.130
you're seeing good contrast between the
two bottom images. You're getting nice,

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:20.090
these are called microtubules -- they're
very bright against a dark background.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:24.920
The one in the middle is less blurry
then the one on the left hand side. So,

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:30.320
blurring makes a little less, or worse
contrast. Then finally, color can add

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:37.660
additional detail of contrast for your
eye. So, let's talk about contrast.

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:41.330
Contrast is whether or not you can see
something in the first place. So, I've

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:45.470
drawn a bunch of dots on this slide and
those represent a signal, a signal that

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:48.650
we want. I put them on top of
different backgrounds and the background

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:52.970
is what we don't care about, or what we
don't really want. So, I've increased the

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:56.510
signal from going from left to right
hand side, and I've increased more and

00:08:56.510 --> 00:09:00.170
more background going from top to bottom.
So, let's take a look at the left hand

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:05.180
side, the weakest signal. You can actually
start to see this weak signal on top of

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:08.630
a black background. But when you've got a
really bright, or white background, that

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:13.340
signal is almost gone. It turns out,
the definition of contrast is

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:18.140
essentially the signal you want minus the
background you don't care about divided by the

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:22.130
background. So, intuitively, what we
think about is that the more background

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:26.300
you have, the weaker the contrast. The
more signal you have, the higher the

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:30.020
contrast. So, the contrast increases from
left to right as we get more and more

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:34.610
signal and it increases from bottom to
top as we get lower and lower

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:39.260
background levels. To give you an example
of how background can effect contrast

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:43.640
tremendously. Stars are actually out
during the day. You just can't see them

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:47.690
because the sunlight is so bright and
that sky is so bright. But, once that

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:51.800
brightness goes away and day turns to
night, the stars, that were the same

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:56.060
signal they were before, are suddenly
visible because the background is no

00:09:56.060 --> 00:10:01.339
longer there. So, background can affect
contrast tremendously. Now that we

00:10:01.339 --> 00:10:05.899
understand contrast, let's talk about
what types of intensity values we're

00:10:05.899 --> 00:10:11.269
going to display from an image. So most
monitors, and printers, and computers

00:10:11.269 --> 00:10:18.320
store images as eight bits of intensity.
Higher grade scientific cameras, or if

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:23.540
you take a high dynamic, or a range, or
HDR photo, you might store those images

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:30.019
as 16 bits, or 16 bits as raw data. But,
most computers can only display eight bits

00:10:30.019 --> 00:10:34.790
of information at any one time. So, what
ranges of values of that 16 bits are you

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:38.300
going to actually choose to display? Well,
if you have something that's really

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:43.760
bright, you might choose the topmost bits,
or the brightest bits to display and

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:48.440
scale those to eight bits that your
monitor, or computer, can display. If that

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:53.810
image is fairly dim, you might take the
dimmest pixels in that image and stretch

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:57.890
them into the eight bits that your monitor can display. But, that

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:01.850
same sort of transformation can also
apply to eight bit images. We can take a

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:06.019
range of those values from an eight bit
image and choose which range to display

00:11:06.019 --> 00:11:12.649
on your monitor. So, in order to
understand what we're doing with this,

00:11:12.649 --> 00:11:16.970
let's talk about how we analyze image
intensity. So, there's actually a way to

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:20.779
plot all of the image intensities in
your image and this is called a

00:11:20.779 --> 00:11:25.279
histogram -- it's a plot of intensity
values. So, what we're going to do, is take

00:11:25.279 --> 00:11:29.690
our mandrill example and that matrix of
numbers from that little tiny portion of

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:34.220
the muzzle and we're going to count the
intensities by range. So, let's take the

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:39.199
range of pixel values from 100 to 119. If
I count those out, go through all that

00:11:39.199 --> 00:11:45.890
and count out, I get about 10 pixels from
that range. If I now go from 120 to 139

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:50.819
and count all those up, I get
thirteen. If I count from 140 to 159 intensity values,

00:11:50.819 --> 00:11:55.110
there's only one of
those, so it's a very small number. From

00:11:55.110 --> 00:12:01.920
160 to 179, I've got about 19 and etc, etc.
You can continue counting in these

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.079
bin ranges. Okay, so what does that
give us? If you'd look at those counts,

00:12:07.079 --> 00:12:11.519
those tally marks, that looks kind of
like a graph. Turn that graph sideways

00:12:11.519 --> 00:12:17.939
and plot it out for the entire image,
going from low intensities on the left,

00:12:17.939 --> 00:12:22.050
or zero, all the way up to 255, to the
highest intensity and we're going to plot

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:26.730
the counts as a graph. This ends up
being called a histogram plot of all the

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:30.569
intensities. So, it's counts over the
entire range of intensities that are

00:12:30.569 --> 00:12:35.670
possible. So, a histogram is actually a
very informative plot of what kind of

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:40.079
intensities are in our image. For
example, for this histogram, we can see

00:12:40.079 --> 00:12:44.220
that there are very few black pixels and
there are very few white pixels. The

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:48.600
majority of these pixels are mostly gray,
centered right about the center of this

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:53.069
image of the intensity values. Let's take
a look at another image. So, this is an

00:12:53.069 --> 00:12:58.860
image of a bunch of boats and we can see
that there is a bunch of high number of

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:02.939
black pixels. So, those black pixels
correspond to the bottoms of those boats.

00:13:02.939 --> 00:13:07.920
There's also a bunch of gray pixels
that correspond to the sea, and the air,

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:12.509
and most of the boat, and a few little
wider and brighter pixels, as well. But,

00:13:12.509 --> 00:13:18.139
this gives us an indication of what kind
of intensity values our images contain.

00:13:18.139 --> 00:13:22.079
Even color histograms can tell us
something. So, I can take all of the

00:13:22.079 --> 00:13:26.100
intensities in the red channel, the green
channel, and the blue channel and plot

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:32.249
those individual histograms. For
example, in the highest values at 255, you

00:13:32.249 --> 00:13:36.209
see a lot of red, and just a little bit
of blue, and no green. Those

00:13:36.209 --> 00:13:39.509
correspond to the bright red stripe
going down the center of the muzzle of

00:13:39.509 --> 00:13:45.540
this mandrill. Just a little bit
below those, there is some fairly bright

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:50.549
blue, with no green, and a tiny little bit
of red, and blue and red together make up

00:13:50.549 --> 00:13:55.620
magenta. So, this is the magenta and blue
stripes along the muzzle next to the

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:59.709
nose.
A little bit farther, or dimmer, you can

00:13:59.709 --> 00:14:03.040
see a bunch of green, and a little bit of
red, and very little blue. This

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:06.490
corresponds to the yellow whiskers,
because red and green together make

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:10.930
yellow. So, we can get a little bit of
information from color from a histogram.

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:17.019
You can do the same sorts of plots
of intensities of color. So, how do we use

00:14:17.019 --> 00:14:21.730
these histogram plots to bring out
information from an image? Well, let's

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:25.089
draw a line going from the bottom left
to the top right corner of this

00:14:25.089 --> 00:14:28.600
histogram. We're going to use this
original histogram as the input and

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:32.740
we're going to create an output
histogram corresponding to the image

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:38.829
that we want. So, the bottom, or zero, in the input, corresponds to

00:14:38.829 --> 00:14:43.899
zero in the output and 255, on the
input, corresponds to 255 in the output.

00:14:43.899 --> 00:14:48.759
We call this transformation a linear
map. We're going linearly from 0 to 255

00:14:48.759 --> 00:14:55.680
on the input, and that maps to 
values from 0 to 255 on the output side.

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:01.300
But, what happens if we stretch this line,
or change the slope of this line? So,

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:05.290
let's take the minimum and raise it up,
and the maximum and lower it down. If

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:10.510
we stretch that image, we essentially,
are narrowing our contrast range to

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:14.350
what's actually in our image, to the
values, or brightness values, that are

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:19.750
actually in our image. So, we can actually
make a better contrast image of this

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:25.959
mandrill. But, we don't have to stick to a
line. We can also use a curve to describe

00:15:25.959 --> 00:15:31.329
a transfer function from input to output.
So, you can use a curve, to the bottom

00:15:31.329 --> 00:15:36.250
right, for instance, to get rid of some of
the darker values, while keeping some of

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:42.130
the brighter values. Or you can use a
curve, to the upper left, to boost the

00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:47.019
darker values without really increasing
the bright values as much. So, we can use

00:15:47.019 --> 00:15:51.490
these tools -- the histogram, and these
transfer functions, and our minimum, our

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:55.959
maximum, and our gamma, to bring out
information and to get the most out of

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:04.870
our scientific images. So, let's use our
newfound knowledge of image intensity

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:08.860
values and histograms to try and bring
out some more information from some

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:12.410
scientific images. So, we're going to be using the

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:16.430
free image manipulation program called
GIMP -- it stands for the "new image

00:16:16.430 --> 00:16:21.110
manipulation program." But, most of the
other software packages you might run

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:27.290
across will have the same sort of
histogram manipulation screens that you

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:31.670
can use to bring out intensity
information and hidden information from

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:39.350
scientific images. So, I'm going to use GIMP
and I'm going to drag one file on to

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:45.410
this image manipulation program. So,
what we have here, are a bunch of stars

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:50.749
taken against a black mountain. Right. So,
let's see if we can bring out some other

00:16:50.749 --> 00:16:58.160
information from this particular photo.
So, we're going to go to the main histogram

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:02.899
window for adjusting and doing these
maps. So, there are two that we want to

00:17:02.899 --> 00:17:06.850
talk about in GIMP. We're going to talk
about, under the colors, menu there's

00:17:06.850 --> 00:17:10.880
something called levels, and there's
something called curves. So, these two

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:14.600
menu items, right here, will let us
manipulate the histogram. So, let's start

00:17:14.600 --> 00:17:19.399
with levels.
Okay, so, levels shows a histogram of the

00:17:19.399 --> 00:17:24.770
intensity values within this image.
So, you can see, this is our histogram. We're

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:31.340
going from dark pixels, to bright pixels
and we're seeing a count of dark to

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:36.020
bright pixels. Now, let's take a look.
There are two basic comps and the first

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:40.940
thing I'm gonna do, is take this maximum
value and drag it down all the way to

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:46.610
about where we start to see stuff. Well,
guess what? Now we can start to see, we've

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:51.770
got these dim stars in the background
and by just changing the intensity

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:55.520
levels, we're able to bring out some of
the information that was hidden to our

00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:59.330
eyes in this image. So, we can now see the
Milky Way, we can now see more

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:03.845
more stars, and we can start to
see this mountain right here.

00:18:03.845 --> 00:18:09.470
Let's bring the intensity down a little
bit more. Now the stars are really

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:14.210
bright, and now, we're starting to see a
little bit of this mountain. So, it looks

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:20.809
like, if we look at this histogram, most
of this line, most of this, comes from the

00:18:20.809 --> 00:18:26.040
mountain, and most of this hump
comes from the stars. So, we could bring

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:29.460
out the stars at the expense of the
mountain and, remember, if we drop the

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:33.330
background down, we can get a little bit
better contrast. So, if we want to focus

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:39.179
on the stars and the Milky Way galaxy
portions, sitting right across your image,

00:18:39.179 --> 00:18:43.919
we can bring the bottom a little bit up
and that decreases the apparent

00:18:43.919 --> 00:18:48.809
background. So I'm just going to zoom in.
I'm going to hit "plus" on the keyboard and

00:18:48.809 --> 00:18:51.710
we can start to see individual stars. All
right.

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:55.590
So, we brought out some hidden
information in this image. Let's take a

00:18:55.590 --> 00:19:04.110
look at a couple of other images. So,
let's take a look at a daytime image.

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:16.830
Let's see if we can actually see some
stars in this and I bet you...(get rid of this and open it up) . I think we

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:21.140
can see anything inside this image. So,
I'm going to bring up, again, colors, levels.

00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:28.200
Right now, this bright stuff here is
showing the sky and the brightest

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:31.860
portions of the image. But, this histogram
is really, really dim it's really hard to

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:35.700
see because the counts are linear.
There's a little button up here and a

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:39.510
lot of your software programs will
have something like this. It makes up

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:43.650
what's called a "logarithmic histogram." So,
this basically expands the histogram out

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:48.809
so you can see more of what's happening.
Again, now this logarithmic histogram,

00:19:48.809 --> 00:19:54.510
if I drag down, we can't
see anything in the dark, because the

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:59.370
bright image is just too bright. Okay so,
we're not going to really be able to pull

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:03.990
out. Let's try dragging the bottom back
up. Okay. Let's change the background,

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:07.290
lower the background. Well, we still
can't see stars in this image and that's

00:20:07.290 --> 00:20:11.970
fine. It's too
little contrast in our original image.

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:16.950
But, let's do one more thing. Let's go to
colors. Reset very quick, just to

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:21.780
get back to normal. I'm going to go to
color curves. Okay, so this, remember that

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:27.630
transfer function we talked about? Well,
we can decrease or increase the minimum

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:35.360
and turn a little bit more black. You can
also change to a curve.

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:40.220
We could potentially make this mountain
a little bit more contrasty by changing

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:44.570
the intensity curve. The fun thing
about this, is that you can actually put

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:51.679
multiple curves on your color
curve, you can put multiple inflection

00:20:51.679 --> 00:20:56.450
points. Okay, but now that we understand
what's happening, it gives us a little bit

00:20:56.450 --> 00:21:01.700
of a better grasp of what we're doing
with these sorts of intensity

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:05.960
transformation curves. All right. So, let's
see if we can bring out something else.

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:12.110
Let's start with another stars image. So,
let's drag another image onto here. Okay.

00:21:12.110 --> 00:21:18.830
So, now, this image is from a 16-bit or
RAW format. So, the previous image right

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:24.440
here was only eight bits and let me take
a look. Let's go to image precision and

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:30.350
this is an eight bit integer image. Okay.
Let's go take a look on this. This, if we

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:34.940
look at image precision, this is 16 bit. So,
there's more information in this image

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:41.059
than there was in the previous image.
Okay, so let's bring up our color levels

00:21:41.059 --> 00:21:44.720
again and take a look. Remember I
still got... this is the normal histogram.

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:48.110
Actually, let's go back to this linear
histogram versus the logarithmic

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:52.730
histogram. Let me click on the linear
histogram right here. Okay, so again, we're

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:58.010
seeing two humps -- one is a black hump and
one is a slightly less black hump. So, let's

00:21:58.010 --> 00:22:03.890
bring our maximum down and,
again, we can start to see the stars.

00:22:03.890 --> 00:22:07.730
So, we can see a lot of interesting
things in here. Again, I can bring the

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:14.870
minimum up and to just increase the
contrast of the sky, of this night sky. So

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:19.460
now we're seeing a little bit better
contrast here of the night sky. But we're

00:22:19.460 --> 00:22:24.380
still washing this out a little bit.
Let's drop this back down. So, a little

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:29.840
bit of sunset left at this twilight
image. But, remember this little hump

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:33.980
right here? Let's move our minimum and
maximum to span that hump and guess what?

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:41.690
Look at that. Now, we see the mountain in
essentially, like daylight conditions.

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:45.950
The sixteen bits of information -- what
that means, is that

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:48.210
we're getting a lot more shades when
we're

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:51.570
looking at this tiny little range of our
input values. We're getting a lot more

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:56.840
shades out because there's more
information stored in the 16 bit image.

00:22:56.840 --> 00:23:03.270
Okay, let's take a look at another image,
Let's see if we can pull out more star

00:23:03.270 --> 00:23:09.270
images from, let's say, an eclipse. So, this
is the Sun during an eclipse and this

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:14.070
is not quite a full eclipse yet. Again, this image, if we take a look at

00:23:14.070 --> 00:23:18.480
the precision, this is an eight bit image. So,
there might not be a lot hidden inside

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:23.280
this image, but we're gonna try anyway.
Let's go to levels and it looks like

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:25.950
there's just a tiny little bit of
information down here. Let's switch to a

00:23:25.950 --> 00:23:30.720
logarithmic histogram. In reality, this is
logarithmic. There's not much information

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:36.620
right here,
but I can still drag this maximum down.

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:43.110
Now, the corona from the Sun is
peeking out a little bit more. We're

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:48.930
seeing more of that. But, in reality, we're
not really seeing much in terms of stars.

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:54.150
This image, the raw data, was
just too dim to get at the stars behind

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:58.080
this image. So, let's try another
eclipse image that's a little bit

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:06.840
farther along. So, this is at
a full eclipse and now this corona is

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:10.860
really bright because this image was
taken at a much longer exposure. Still,

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:15.560
it's an eight bit image but let's see what
we can bring out with the levels. Okay,

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:23.540
let's bring our maximum down. Okay? Now we're starting to see some things.

00:24:23.540 --> 00:24:27.150
Here's something else I want to
point out. So, we're starting to see some

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:31.760
spots in the background. I am going to
have to zoom up to take a look at these.

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:38.490
I can hold down the spacebar
and use this to drag our image around. I

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:44.040
can use the plus key to zoom in, and the
minus key to zoom out, and the spacebar

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:47.880
to drag everything around. Okay, so now,
we're starting to see blobs right here.

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:52.950
This is a star. You can zoom up on it.
And I can tell that this is likely a

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:58.350
star because it takes up more than one
pixel. You notice in the background area

00:24:58.350 --> 00:25:01.980
right here, there's a bunch of noise and
this is what happens with

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:06.690
low-light images. Your cameras just
generate a lot of noise and that tends

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:11.100
to be really low values of intensity, but
it's still there. When you're blowing up

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:16.860
the intensity like this, that noise comes
right out. You'll notice that

00:25:16.860 --> 00:25:20.790
there are single points of noise right
here. It looks like individual pixels

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:24.840
might be bright surrounded by darker
things. That's an

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:29.640
indication that what that is, is noise --
things that tend to look square. Whereas,

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:34.679
do you see this, right here? This blob?
That is likely a star. This blob, right

00:25:34.679 --> 00:25:38.520
here, is likely a star because it takes
up more than one pixel. It's taking up a

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:43.710
few pixels. So, that is a collection of
photons, a very dim collection of photons,

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:46.799
but a collection of photons, nonetheless,
that indicate that what you're looking

00:25:46.799 --> 00:25:52.530
at is a star, and not noise. So, again,
there's less clarity to

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:58.700
this image because the signal here is
very weak, and the noise is very strong.

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:04.760
So, now let's look at a biological
image -- something a little bit less

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:09.990
astronomy. So, this is an image of a
flower and let's see if we can find

00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:15.240
something else on this flower. So, I'm
going to go to color level. Let's take a

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:19.590
look at our histogram. It's a little bit
low, so I'm going to switch to a logarithmic,

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:24.360
so I could see more of the values right
here. Okay. Typically what I do, when

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:28.470
I'm looking in a scientific image, is I
will play around with the minimum, and

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:32.210
I'll drag that up and down, and see if I
can see anything new.

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:36.450
There's something that's poking out.
I can drag the maximum

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:41.250
down a little bit and see if I could see
anything at the dim portions. Okay,

00:26:41.250 --> 00:26:48.990
so, let's drag up this minimum. You're
starting to see something with a little

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:55.620
bit more contrast. It looks like there's a
caterpillar on this flower. We can

00:26:55.620 --> 00:27:00.540
bring it out with just
manipulating the histogram and this

00:27:00.540 --> 00:27:06.510
transfer function, the input versus
output levels. Similarly, let's take a

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:11.200
look at these petals. So, I'm going to
zoom up on a petal.

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:15.519
These petals are fairly dark. They're
darker than the center of the flower.

00:27:15.519 --> 00:27:19.690
Let's zoom back out. So, the petals here
surrounding, and even this black space,

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:23.230
the flower is really bright. But
sometimes, if you're looking for

00:27:23.230 --> 00:27:26.919
something really bright in an image, it's
going to overwhelm the dark stuff that

00:27:26.919 --> 00:27:32.950
you might be interested in seeing. So, I'm
going to drag this top portion down and see

00:27:32.950 --> 00:27:39.130
if I can bring out some detail at the
darker levels. Now, at the darker levels,

00:27:39.130 --> 00:27:45.429
you're starting to see, potentially,
pollen on this flower. You can see

00:27:45.429 --> 00:27:50.529
the veins on the flower
petals a little bit clearer.

00:27:50.529 --> 00:27:56.799
And let's just compare that to dragging all the way up. So, sometimes the

00:27:56.799 --> 00:28:02.350
information you're looking at, is hidden
in dark information. You can

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:07.809
sacrifice bright pixels, and bright
portions of your data, in order to look

00:28:07.809 --> 00:28:12.669
at the dimmer stuff. So, that will,
hopefully, give you a little bit of an

00:28:12.669 --> 00:28:18.190
idea of how to bring information out of
scientific photos. To look for the hidden

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:22.840
side in the images you took and to
understand what image intensity levels,

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:28.360
and histograms, and these transfer
functions can show you with scientific

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:34.240
images. [Brian] Thank you for joining us for this
tutorial in our "Imaging Science Series"

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:39.669
with the MIT Museum and Koch Institute.
We'd love to see your stunning science

00:28:39.669 --> 00:28:43.600
photos. If you'd like to share them with
us you can share them on Facebook, Twitter,

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:50.559
and Instagram and use the hashtag #MITMImagingScience to share those with us.

00:28:50.559 --> 00:28:56.679
At the end of this program, so on June
20th, we'll be holding a webinar to look

00:28:56.679 --> 00:29:01.870
at what makes a great science photo and
some of those submissions have a chance

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:07.720
to be featured during that webinar. For
